29
00:14:46,360 --> 00:14:47,460
Rep. Brian Poe: Good afternoon and welcome.

30
00:14:48,200 --> 00:14:54,620
Rep. Brian Poe: Today, we take up the fiscal year 2027 National Expenditures Program of the Department of

31
00:14:54,620 --> 00:14:55,420
Rep. Brian Poe: Information and Communications.

32
00:14:55,420 --> 00:14:59,440
Rep. Brian Poe: Technology and its attached agencies.

33
00:14:59,440 --> 00:15:05,540
Rep. Brian Poe: We assess this budget at an important juncture for the country. In just a few years, the government has built

34
00:15:05,540 --> 00:15:12,380
Rep. Brian Poe: a stronger legal foundation for a connected Philippines. The E-Governance Act advances

35
00:15:12,380 --> 00:15:19,900
Rep. Brian Poe: digital government and integrated public services. The Conectadong Pinoy Act seeks to expand access

36
00:15:19,900 --> 00:15:26,600
Rep. Brian Poe: and competition in data transmission and connectivity. But passing laws is only the

37
00:15:26,600 --> 00:15:34,720
Rep. Brian Poe: beginning. This budget must turn those laws into actual service. For me, the first test remains

38
00:15:34,720 --> 00:15:41,220
Rep. Brian Poe: connectivity. We will therefore be looking closely at the connectivity targets presented to Congress.

39
00:15:41,820 --> 00:15:48,740
Rep. Brian Poe: We want to know not only how many sites, access points, kilometers of fiber, or institutions are

40
00:15:48,740 --> 00:15:54,700
Rep. Brian Poe: reported as connected, but whether or not people on the ground are actually receiving reliable,

41
00:15:55,340 --> 00:15:56,580
Rep. Brian Poe: usable, and affordable services.

42
00:15:56,600 --> 00:16:05,620
Rep. Brian Poe: This is particularly important for our public schools. Connectivity in schools should mean more

43
00:16:05,620 --> 00:16:11,920
Rep. Brian Poe: than being able to just say that the school has been connected. We have to ask, is the connection

44
00:16:11,920 --> 00:16:19,060
Rep. Brian Poe: actually working? Is the bandwidth sufficient for teachers and students? Is it reliable throughout

45
00:16:19,060 --> 00:16:25,680
Rep. Brian Poe: the school day and cannot support digital learning, online government systems, and increasing

46
00:16:25,680 --> 00:16:26,580
Rep. Brian Poe: technological resources?

47
00:16:26,600 --> 00:16:37,380
Rep. Brian Poe: In short, we have to validate whether our target translates into real connectivity.

48
00:16:37,380 --> 00:16:45,220
Rep. Brian Poe: Our challenge is not just how many sites are connected to the report. The question is whether

49
00:16:45,220 --> 00:16:55,520
Rep. Brian Poe: teachers and students use it every day. And as we connect more Filipinos, we must also confront a

50
00:16:55,520 --> 00:16:56,280
Rep. Brian Poe: second question.

51
00:16:56,600 --> 00:17:03,040
Rep. Brian Poe: Where does our data go? Who controls the infrastructure carrying it? And how well are we

52
00:17:03,040 --> 00:17:09,560
Rep. Brian Poe: protecting it? Data sovereignty is no longer an abstract technological issue. Government is

53
00:17:09,560 --> 00:17:16,180
Rep. Brian Poe: digitizing records, transactions, identities, payments, and public services. We're generating

54
00:17:16,180 --> 00:17:23,180
Rep. Brian Poe: and processing enormous amounts of sensitive government data, citizen data. And so we need

55
00:17:23,180 --> 00:17:26,340
Rep. Brian Poe: to understand where critical government data is stored.

56
00:17:26,600 --> 00:17:42,140
Rep. Brian Poe: We need to understand where infrastructure is, what infrastructure carries it, and what safeguards apply when cloud and data services are provided by foreign entities and whether government has adequate control over information that is critical to national security and public administration.

57
00:17:42,140 --> 00:17:55,000
Rep. Brian Poe: This brings us to the expanding role of the DICT. The Department can no longer be viewed as a simple government agency responsible for internet connectivity and information technology projects.

58
00:17:56,600 --> 00:18:04,440
Rep. Brian Poe: In this new age, DICT sits at the intersection of connectivity, cybersecurity, artificial

59
00:18:04,440 --> 00:18:11,620
Rep. Brian Poe: intelligence, digital government, data governance, transparency, and economic development.

60
00:18:11,620 --> 00:18:14,060
Rep. Brian Poe: Cybersecurity is now national security.

61
00:18:14,060 --> 00:18:19,720
Rep. Brian Poe: Every government system we put online creates both an opportunity for better public service

62
00:18:19,720 --> 00:18:21,960
Rep. Brian Poe: and a potential for attack.

63
00:18:21,960 --> 00:18:27,760
Rep. Brian Poe: The question for this committee is whether we are investing enough, not only in systems

64
00:18:27,760 --> 00:18:33,560
Rep. Brian Poe: but in people, defenses, standards, and institutions to protect them.

65
00:18:33,560 --> 00:18:35,920
Rep. Brian Poe: Artificial intelligence presents another challenge.

66
00:18:35,920 --> 00:18:39,660
Rep. Brian Poe: AI can make government faster and more efficient.

67
00:18:39,660 --> 00:18:45,360
Rep. Brian Poe: It can support education, health, disaster response, agriculture, business, and many

68
00:18:45,360 --> 00:18:46,760
Rep. Brian Poe: other sectors.

69
00:18:46,760 --> 00:18:50,940
Rep. Brian Poe: But government must also be prepared for its risks.

70
00:18:50,940 --> 00:18:51,940
Rep. Brian Poe: From deepfakes to automated fraud,

71
00:18:51,940 --> 00:19:00,120
Rep. Brian Poe: to privacy issues, cybersecurity, accountability, and the possible disruption of Filipino jobs.

72
00:19:00,120 --> 00:19:06,140
Rep. Brian Poe: Our approach cannot simply be to fear AI or to regulate it without understanding.

73
00:19:06,140 --> 00:19:11,800
Rep. Brian Poe: The Philippines should be positioning itself to use AI, govern AI responsibly, and create

74
00:19:11,800 --> 00:19:16,620
Rep. Brian Poe: opportunities for Filipinos in AI-driven economies.

75
00:19:16,620 --> 00:19:20,920
Rep. Brian Poe: The same digital transformation should strengthen transparency.

76
00:19:20,920 --> 00:19:25,380
Rep. Brian Poe: Technology should make government easier to scrutinize, not harder.

77
00:19:25,380 --> 00:19:31,480
Rep. Brian Poe: Digital systems should allow citizens to know how public money is spent, how government

78
00:19:31,480 --> 00:19:37,660
Rep. Brian Poe: decisions are made, and whether agencies are delivering what they promised.

79
00:19:37,660 --> 00:19:43,340
Rep. Brian Poe: If we are spending billions of pesos to digitize the government, the greater accountability

80
00:19:43,340 --> 00:19:49,100
Rep. Brian Poe: and transparency should be among the returns of that investment.

81
00:19:49,100 --> 00:19:50,100
Rep. Brian Poe: Finally.

82
00:19:50,100 --> 00:19:53,100
Rep. Brian Poe: Digital policy is jobs policy.

83
00:19:53,100 --> 00:20:00,580
Rep. Brian Poe: Reliable broadband, competitive digital infrastructure, cloud services, cybersecurity, data centers,

84
00:20:00,580 --> 00:20:06,740
Rep. Brian Poe: digital platforms, and emerging technologies can attract investment and create higher-value

85
00:20:06,740 --> 00:20:07,840
Rep. Brian Poe: employment.

86
00:20:07,840 --> 00:20:13,660
Rep. Brian Poe: Our objective should not be to just make Filipinos consumers of technology.

87
00:20:13,660 --> 00:20:19,560
Rep. Brian Poe: We should be building conditions for Filipinos to become developers, cybersecurity experts,

88
00:20:19,560 --> 00:20:24,000
Rep. Brian Poe: engineers, entrepreneurs, innovators, and creators of technology.

89
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:29,240
Rep. Brian Poe: Ang digital transformation ay hindi lang tungkol sa mas mabilis na internet.

90
00:20:29,240 --> 00:20:35,500
Rep. Brian Poe: Dapat may trabaho, negosyo, oportunidad at mas mahusay na serbisyo na kasunod nito.

91
00:20:35,500 --> 00:20:41,640
Rep. Brian Poe: But opportunity must come with trust.

92
00:20:41,640 --> 00:20:47,400
Rep. Brian Poe: As more Filipinos transact, communicate, work, study, and receive government services online,

93
00:20:47,400 --> 00:20:48,400
Rep. Brian Poe: we also have to make

94
00:20:49,400 --> 00:20:56,440
